I am going to give you some text selected by a reader while reading "{{ document.title}}" written by {{ document.author }} followed by a question they asked.

Your job is to guess whether the question they asked is either Random or Document-Specific and then venture a one-sentence reply.

Random: A Random question is any question a reader might have that is not directly related to or answered by the specific content of the document they are reading, and instead requires your built-in, external knowledge of the world. These questions arise from curiosity or external thoughts. For example, "What is the capital of France?", "How do solar panels work?", or "What are the health benefits of yoga?" would all be Random questions. A Random question might be inspired by something mentioned in the selected text, but asking for external, encyclopedic knowledge about something selected is a Random question. Another example of a Random question that references something in the selected text, but is Random and NOT Document-Specific would be a question about the etymology of the word. There's almost no way most documents contain the origin of a word. Sometimes there will be a previous chat history of the reader asking questions before the current question. That follow-up question is usually Random, but again, the classification as Random versus Document-Specific is based on whether the answer is more likely to be contained in your built-in knowledge (Random) or in the document (Document-Specific).

Document-Specific: A Document-Specific question is a question directly related to the internal content of the document the reader is engaging with and requires searching the document for context. These questions arise from the reader's need to understand or explore the specific text further. Document-Specific questions typically seek factual answers contained in the text or non-factual intepretations of the text. Ask for you for encyclopedic knowledge about some entity mentioned in the text is NOT Document-Specific since those answers are neither answered by the text nor are interpretations of the text. They require your external, built-in knowledge. Expansion or interpretation of what an author means by a certain term or phrase would be a Document-Specific question since this requires reading into the text. Sometimes there will be a previous chat history of the reader asking questions before the current question. That follow-up question is usually Random, but can be Document-Specific if the answer is more likely to be contained in the document.

